return await window.__createIslands([{"id":"ess-30","kind":"prose","x":3200,"y":600,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>An apparatus is often imagined as a discrete object: a device with an input, output, housing, and function. But in these works, apparatus expands into atmosphere. A transmitter alters the status of a room; a receiver changes what silence means; a projection thickens air; a simulation turns architecture into model; a seismometer links terrestrial movement to cosmic address; a radio signal leaving orbit makes the boundary of the work difficult to name. Apparatus becomes distributed across site, device, field, and interpretation. This expansion is not merely technical. It changes the ontology of the artwork. The work is no longer a bounded object encountered by a viewer, but a condition of possible reception distributed among instruments, energies, environments, and temporalities. Such a condition may include visible components, but its decisive operations are often elsewhere. They occur in tuning, conversion, propagation, modelling, grounding, delay, and decay. To move from apparatus to atmosphere is to recognise that the work cannot be fully separated from the medium through which it acts. The medium is not a channel added after the fact. It is part of the work's body, if body remains the right word.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-31","kind":"prose","x":5200,"y":-400,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>The seismometer listens to movements that may be too small, too distant, or too differently scaled for the body. It translates terrestrial instability into data, and in doing so redistributes authorship. The artist does not produce the signal alone. The Earth moves; the instrument measures; the system converts; the transmitter sends; the wave travels; the receiving possibility remains open. This is not collaboration with nature in a romantic sense. It is a technical compact with planetary movement. The work arises from arranging the conditions under which an environmental event can become signal, and under which signal can become artistic proposition. Such redistribution of authorship is important because it shifts attention away from expressive intention and toward operative chains. The artist becomes less origin than organiser of relays. The seismometer is not a neutral tool but a participant in the production of form. It registers according to its own capacities and limitations, producing an account of Earth that is already selective, already formatted. To treat the seismometer as author is not to personify the device, but to acknowledge that the work's agency is distributed across technical and terrestrial processes.</p></div>"},{"id":"ess-32","kind":"prose","x":5800,"y":-600,"w":460,"html":"<div style=\"font-family:'nimbus sans l',sans-serif;font-size:14px;line-height:1.5;color:#000\"><p>Messages sent into space often invite fantasies of extraterrestrial reception, as if the meaning of transmission depended on eventual recognition by another intelligence. But reception by aliens is not required for a planetary address to matter. The address is planetary before it is interstellar. It says that Earth can be technically exteriorised, that its movements can be modulated into radio, that its cultural practices can take the form of outward-travelling waves. This is not a postcard to elsewhere. It is Earth becoming signal. The distinction is important because it shifts the work away from communication understood as message delivery and toward communication understood as energetic relation. A signal may matter even if no one receives it, just as a field may operate without spectacle, and radiation may persist without spectators. The planetary address is therefore not primarily semantic. It is ontological and aesthetic. It marks the possibility that art can occur as an outward modification of a field, as an event whose scale exceeds the conditions of exhibition, and whose addressee may remain structurally uncertain.</p></div>"}]);